{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "יברכך ה' אלהיך בכל מעשה ידך", :translit "y'varek'kha H' Elohekha b'khol ma'asekh yadekha", :gloss "YHWH your Elohim will bless you in all the work of your hands"} {:he "וזה דבר השמטה שמוט", :translit "v'zeh davar ha-sh'mittah sh'mot", :gloss "and this is the matter of the release: you shall release"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhah parshiyot: the phrase about blessing the work of your hands is immediately followed by the release (shemittah) text, linking them thematically."}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'YHWH your Elohim will bless you in all the work of your hands' appears in Deuteronomy 14:29 in the context of giving the tithe to the Levite, stranger, orphan, and widow. The adjacent verse Deuteronomy 15:1–2 opens the laws of shemittah (the remission of debts). The juxtaposition is taken to teach that the blessing of 'all the work of your hands' is connected to observance of the release, and that the creditor must formally declare to the debtor, 'I am releasing,' as indicated by the phrase 'this is the matter of the release: you shall release' (Deuteronomy 15:1–2).", :sources ["Deuteronomy 14:29" "Deuteronomy 15:1–2"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhah parshiyot: the phrase about blessing the work of your hands is immediately followed by the release (shemittah) text, linking them thematically.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:36:42.390649974Z", :model "glm-5.2"}